@@ -69,6 +69,26 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as a string with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON with syntax highlighting, and the ``indent`` argument sets the number of spaces to indent (default 4). Set ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting. Here's an example::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+You can also log a JSON renderable with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, or import the :func:`~rich.print_json` function from the package to print with the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "rich"}', indent=2)
+
+The JSON renderable is available as :class:`~rich.json.JSON` if you want to print it with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` yourself.
+
+Printed JSON is not word wrapped. The renderable sets ``no_wrap``, so a long line is kept on one line.
+
+From the command line, you can pretty print a JSON file by running the json module with a path::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
